    "origin": "",
    "establishes": "A submission by counsel is a party's assertion made in court. It is neither a finding nor evidence that no such order exists, and it does not establish an absence. What it establishes is that the absence was asserted, on the record, by counsel who had looked. The archive's own position is unchanged: no order authorising force has been located, and 'not located' is not 'does not exist'. The count of orders found on the front page stays at zero for the same reason it was zero yesterday. What would settle it: the affidavit the Union was directed to file disclosing any standing orders. That affidavit, if filed, either produces an order or records its absence on oath, and either outcome is a document this archive would hold. One publication only, so REPORTED. The 2011 SOP and the 2016 report are separately worth obtaining: see OQ-044.",
