    "establishes": "What is established here is that the hearing happened and what it found. That does not make the findings true. A fact-finding panel has no judicial standing, and its conclusions are not evidence of the events. They are the considered account of named people who heard witnesses. The archive records them as that, with the panel named so a reader can weigh it. This is the first account of Bihar that is not the State's. Until now the record for Bihar was almost entirely the government describing itself. Three things it adds that nothing else here had: Sitamarhi, where police are said to have entered a welfare hostel on the evening of 21 July and beaten students, a place and date this archive did not hold; the figure of 64 FIRs; and the reported detention of two PUCL lawyers assisting detained protesters, who are said to have been charged and paraded handcuffed. Sitamarhi is not yet its own incident. Siwan was opened as a separate incident because a State affidavit put it beyond doubt. Sitamarhi rests on one panel's account, and opening an incident on that alone would be a lower standard than the one applied to Siwan. It is recorded here and will be opened when a second, independent account or a document supports it. See OQ-050. The report itself is not held: what the archive has is three outlets' accounts of it. See OQ-049.",
